"""Original SAA-C03-style questions written for Cloud Passport (not exam questions).
Row: (chapter, stem, options, correct indices, explanation). Append only: ids are positional.
"""
EXTRA = [
# ---------- App Integration ----------
("integ", "An order service publishes events that must be processed by three independent systems: billing, shipping and analytics. Each system must receive every event and must be able to fail without affecting the others. Which design meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 ["Publish to an Amazon SNS topic that fans out to three Amazon SQS queues, one per system", "Publish to a single Amazon SQS queue that all three systems poll", "Write events to an Amazon S3 bucket and have each system list new objects every minute", "Have the order service call each system's API directly in sequence"], [0],
 "**SNS + SQS fan-out** delivers a copy of every message to each queue, and each queue buffers independently, so one slow or failed consumer doesn't affect the others.\n\n- A single SQS queue gives each message to only one consumer.\n- S3 polling and direct calls add custom code and tight coupling."),
("integ", "A payment workflow must process messages exactly once and strictly in the order they were sent for each customer. Throughput is about 200 messages per second. Which solution should a solutions architect choose?",
 ["Amazon SQS standard queue", "Amazon SQS FIFO queue with the customer ID as the message group ID", "Amazon SNS standard topic", "Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"], [1],
 "**SQS FIFO** gives exactly-once processing and ordering **within a message group**. Using the customer ID as the group ID keeps per-customer order while allowing parallelism across customers. 200 msg/s is well within FIFO limits.\n\n- Standard queues are at-least-once and best-effort ordering.\n- Firehose is a delivery service, not an ordered work queue."),
("integ", "Workers pull jobs from an Amazon SQS queue. Some jobs take up to 10 minutes, and the team notices the same job is sometimes processed twice. What is the MOST likely fix?",
 ["Enable long polling on the queue", "Increase the visibility timeout to longer than the maximum processing time", "Decrease the message retention period", "Switch to an SNS topic"], [1],
 "If processing takes longer than the **visibility timeout**, the message becomes visible again and another worker picks it up. Set the timeout above the longest job (or extend it with ChangeMessageVisibility).\n\n- Long polling only reduces empty receives."),
("integ", "Messages that repeatedly fail processing are blocking an SQS-based pipeline and wasting compute. The team wants to keep the failed messages for later analysis. What should they configure?",
 ["A dead-letter queue with a maxReceiveCount redrive policy", "A shorter visibility timeout", "Message deduplication IDs", "An SNS subscription filter policy"], [0],
 "A **dead-letter queue (DLQ)** receives messages after they've been received `maxReceiveCount` times without being deleted. This moves poison messages aside for inspection without blocking the main queue."),
("integ", "A company wants to coordinate a multi-step order process: validate payment, reserve inventory, and send a confirmation, with retries and a compensating step if inventory fails. Developers want a visual workflow and minimal custom orchestration code. Which service fits best?",
 ["AWS Step Functions", "Amazon SQS", "Amazon EventBridge Scheduler", "AWS Batch"], [0],
 "**Step Functions** orchestrates steps as a state machine with built-in retries, catch/compensation branches and a visual console.\n\n- SQS decouples but doesn't orchestrate.\n- EventBridge Scheduler triggers on a schedule; Batch runs batch compute jobs."),
("integ", "A SaaS company wants to react to events from a third-party SaaS provider and from its own AWS services, routing each event type to different targets using content-based rules. Which service should be used?",
 ["Amazon EventBridge", "Amazon SQS", "Amazon MQ", "AWS AppSync"], [0],
 "**EventBridge** is an event bus with partner (SaaS) integrations and rules that match on event content and route to many targets.\n\n- Amazon MQ is for migrating apps that use existing protocols such as JMS/AMQP/MQTT."),
("integ", "A company is migrating an on-premises application that uses Apache ActiveMQ with JMS. They want a managed broker without rewriting messaging code. What should they use?",
 ["Amazon SQS", "Amazon SNS", "Amazon MQ", "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"], [2],
 "**Amazon MQ** is managed ActiveMQ/RabbitMQ, so existing JMS, AMQP, STOMP or MQTT code keeps working. SQS/SNS would require code changes."),
("integ", "An API receives unpredictable traffic spikes that overwhelm the backend database writes. Users can tolerate a few seconds of delay before their data is saved. Which change BEST protects the database?",
 ["Put an Amazon SQS queue between the API and a worker that writes to the database at a controlled rate", "Increase the database instance size permanently", "Add Amazon CloudFront in front of the API", "Enable Multi-AZ on the database"], [0],
 "A queue **buffers write spikes** (load leveling) so workers write at a steady rate. Bigger instances cost more all the time; CloudFront caches reads, not writes; Multi-AZ is for availability, not throughput."),
# ---------- IAM ----------
("iam", "A company uses AWS Organizations. The security team must ensure that no account, including account administrators, can disable AWS CloudTrail. What should they use?",
 ["An IAM policy attached to each administrator", "A service control policy (SCP) that denies cloudtrail:StopLogging and cloudtrail:DeleteTrail", "AWS Config managed rules", "A permissions boundary on the root user"], [1],
 "**SCPs** set the maximum permissions for every principal in member accounts, including administrators, and an explicit deny can't be overridden by IAM policies.\n\n- AWS Config detects changes but doesn't prevent them.\n- Note: SCPs don't affect the management account."),
("iam", "Developers must be allowed to create IAM roles for their Lambda functions, but those roles must never be able to grant more permissions than a defined set. What is the BEST approach?",
 ["Require developers to attach a permissions boundary to every role they create", "Give developers AdministratorAccess and review CloudTrail weekly", "Use an S3 bucket policy", "Create one shared role for all functions"], [0],
 "A **permissions boundary** caps the maximum permissions an identity can have. Combined with a condition that requires the boundary on `iam:CreateRole`, developers can self-serve without escalating privileges."),
("iam", "A company has 40 AWS accounts and wants employees to sign in once with their corporate identity provider and get access to the accounts they need. Which solution requires the LEAST ongoing effort?",
 ["Create IAM users in every account", "AWS IAM Identity Center connected to the corporate IdP", "Share one IAM user per team", "Amazon Cognito user pools"], [1],
 "**IAM Identity Center** (formerly AWS SSO) gives central, multi-account single sign-on with permission sets, integrated with external IdPs.\n\n- Cognito is for customers of your apps, not workforce access to AWS accounts."),
("iam", "A mobile app needs to let its end users upload photos directly to Amazon S3 after they sign in with social identity providers. What should the architect use to provide temporary, scoped AWS credentials?",
 ["Embed an IAM user's access keys in the app", "Amazon Cognito identity pools", "IAM Identity Center", "An S3 bucket policy allowing public writes"], [1],
 "**Cognito identity pools** exchange a signed-in user's token for temporary AWS credentials tied to an IAM role, which can be scoped (for example, to the user's own S3 prefix). Never embed long-term keys in apps."),
("iam", "Account A needs an application to read objects from an S3 bucket in Account B. Which TWO steps are required when using cross-account IAM roles? (Choose TWO)",
 ["Create a role in Account B with S3 read permissions and a trust policy allowing Account A", "Grant the application in Account A permission to call sts:AssumeRole on that role", "Copy Account B's root credentials into Account A", "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration", "Make the bucket public"], [0, 1],
 "Cross-account role access needs both sides: **the role's trust policy** (Account B trusts Account A) and **permission in Account A** to `sts:AssumeRole`. The app then receives temporary credentials."),
("iam", "An auditor asks which S3 buckets and KMS keys in the account are shared with external principals. Which service gives this with the LEAST effort?",
 ["IAM Access Analyzer", "AWS Trusted Advisor", "Amazon Inspector", "AWS Artifact"], [0],
 "**IAM Access Analyzer** analyzes resource policies and reports resources shared outside your account or organization.\n\n- Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities.\n- Artifact provides compliance reports."),
# ---------- Storage ----------
("storage", "A company stores logs in Amazon S3. Logs are read often for 30 days, rarely for the next 11 months, and must be kept for 7 years for compliance with retrieval within 48 hours acceptable. Which configuration is MOST cost-effective?",
 ["S3 Standard, then a lifecycle rule to S3 Standard-IA at 30 days and S3 Glacier Deep Archive at 365 days", "S3 Standard for 7 years", "S3 One Zone-IA from day 1", "S3 Intelligent-Tiering with no lifecycle rules"], [0],
 "Lifecycle transitions match the access pattern: **Standard → Standard-IA (30 days minimum) → Deep Archive** (cheapest, 12–48 h retrieval) for long retention. One Zone-IA risks compliance data if an AZ is lost."),
("storage", "Objects in an S3 bucket must not be deleted or overwritten by anyone, including the root user, for 5 years to meet regulatory requirements. What should be enabled?",
 ["S3 Versioning only", "S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a 5-year retention period", "S3 Object Lock in governance mode", "MFA Delete"], [1],
 "**Object Lock compliance mode** prevents deletion or overwrite by any user, including root, until retention expires (WORM).\n\n- Governance mode can be bypassed by users with special permission.\n- MFA Delete adds friction but doesn't enforce retention."),
("storage", "A Windows-based application on EC2 needs a shared SMB file system integrated with Microsoft Active Directory. Which service should be used?",
 ["Amazon EFS", "Amazon FSx for Windows File Server", "Amazon FSx for Lustre", "Amazon S3 File Gateway"], [1],
 "**FSx for Windows File Server** provides native SMB shares with AD integration.\n\n- EFS is NFS for Linux.\n- FSx for Lustre is for high-performance computing workloads."),
("storage", "A high-performance computing job needs a file system with hundreds of GB/s throughput that reads its input from an S3 bucket and writes results back to S3. Which service fits BEST?",
 ["Amazon EFS with Max I/O", "Amazon FSx for Lustre linked to the S3 bucket", "Amazon EBS io2 volumes", "AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway"], [1],
 "**FSx for Lustre** is built for HPC and ML, with sub-millisecond latency and very high throughput. It can link to an S3 bucket to lazy-load inputs and export results."),
("storage", "A database on EC2 needs a block volume with 64,000 IOPS and sub-millisecond latency, attached to a single Nitro-based instance. Which EBS volume type should be used?",
 ["gp3", "io2 Block Express", "st1", "sc1"], [1],
 "**io2 Block Express** supports up to 256,000 IOPS with sub-millisecond latency for demanding databases.\n\n- gp3 tops out at 16,000 IOPS.\n- st1/sc1 are HDD throughput or cold volumes."),
("storage", "Users around the world upload large files (several GB) to a single S3 bucket in us-east-1 and complain about slow uploads. Which TWO actions improve upload performance? (Choose TWO)",
 ["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration", "Use multipart upload", "Change the storage class to S3 One Zone-IA", "Enable S3 Versioning", "Enable default encryption with SSE-KMS"], [0, 1],
 "**Transfer Acceleration** routes uploads through CloudFront edge locations over the AWS backbone. **Multipart upload** sends parts in parallel and retries only failed parts. Storage class, versioning and encryption don't speed up uploads."),
("storage", "An on-premises application writes files to an NFS share. The company wants those files stored as objects in Amazon S3 while keeping a local cache for low-latency access. Which service should be used?",
 ["AWS DataSync", "Amazon S3 File Gateway", "AWS Snowball Edge", "Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP"], [1],
 "**S3 File Gateway** (Storage Gateway) presents NFS/SMB on premises, stores files as S3 objects, and caches recent data locally.\n\n- DataSync is for one-time or scheduled transfers, not a live mount."),
("storage", "A bucket's data must be encrypted at rest, and the security team needs an audit trail of every key use plus the ability to rotate and disable keys. Which option meets these requirements?",
 ["SSE-S3", "SSE-KMS with a customer managed key", "Client-side encryption with keys stored in the application", "No encryption; use bucket policies"], [1],
 "**SSE-KMS with a customer managed key** logs every use of the key in CloudTrail and lets you control rotation, key policy and disabling.\n\n- SSE-S3 keys are fully managed by S3, with no per-key audit or control."),
# ---------- Databases ----------
("db", "An application on Amazon RDS for MySQL has heavy read traffic for reports that slows down writes. The reports can tolerate slightly stale data. What is the MOST cost-effective way to improve performance?",
 ["Enable Multi-AZ", "Create read replicas and point reporting queries at them", "Upgrade to a larger instance class", "Migrate to DynamoDB"], [1],
 "**Read replicas** offload reads using asynchronous replication (slight lag is acceptable here).\n\n- Multi-AZ standby instances don't serve reads in the classic deployment; Multi-AZ is for high availability."),
("db", "A gaming company needs a database that serves millions of requests per second with single-digit millisecond latency and a flexible key-value schema. Which service should they use?",
 ["Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL", "Amazon DynamoDB", "Amazon Redshift", "Amazon Neptune"], [1],
 "**DynamoDB** is serverless key-value/document storage with consistent single-digit-ms latency at any scale. Add DAX for microsecond reads.\n\n- Redshift is analytics; Neptune is a graph database."),
("db", "A global application needs a relational database with reads served locally in three Regions and recovery in under a minute if the primary Region fails. Which solution fits BEST?",
 ["Amazon Aurora Global Database", "RDS Multi-AZ in one Region", "DynamoDB global tables", "Cross-Region RDS snapshots copied daily"], [0],
 "**Aurora Global Database** replicates to secondary Regions with typically under a second of lag. A secondary can be promoted in about a minute (RTO) for relational workloads.\n\n- DynamoDB global tables are NoSQL.\n- Daily snapshots give a far worse RPO and RTO."),
("db", "A DynamoDB table has unpredictable traffic that is idle most of the day with occasional large spikes. The company wants to avoid capacity planning. Which capacity mode should be used?",
 ["Provisioned capacity with auto scaling", "On-demand capacity", "Provisioned capacity with reserved capacity", "DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)"], [1],
 "**On-demand mode** bills per request and handles sudden spikes without capacity planning, which suits unpredictable or spiky workloads. Provisioned mode with auto scaling is cheaper for steady, predictable traffic."),
("db", "An application's database credentials must be rotated automatically every 30 days without code changes to the rotation logic. Which service should store the credentials?",
 ["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store standard parameters", "AWS Secrets Manager", "An encrypted S3 object", "Environment variables in EC2 user data"], [1],
 "**Secrets Manager** has built-in automatic rotation for RDS, Aurora, Redshift and DocumentDB credentials. Parameter Store doesn't rotate secrets natively."),
("db", "A web application repeatedly runs the same expensive queries against Amazon RDS, and session data must be shared across several web servers. Which service addresses BOTH needs?",
 ["Amazon ElastiCache", "Amazon RDS Proxy", "Amazon CloudFront", "Amazon S3"], [0],
 "**ElastiCache** (Redis or Memcached) caches query results and stores session state centrally. RDS Proxy pools connections but doesn't cache results."),
("db", "Many AWS Lambda functions open new connections to an Amazon RDS database during traffic spikes, exhausting the connection limit. What is the BEST fix?",
 ["Increase the Lambda timeout", "Use Amazon RDS Proxy", "Enable Multi-AZ", "Move the database to a public subnet"], [1],
 "**RDS Proxy** pools and shares database connections, which protects the database from connection storms caused by many concurrent Lambda invocations."),
# ---------- Networking ----------
("net", "EC2 instances in private subnets need to download software updates from the internet, but must not be reachable from the internet. What should be deployed for high availability?",
 ["One NAT gateway in a single public subnet", "A NAT gateway in each Availability Zone's public subnet, with each private route table pointing to its own AZ's NAT gateway", "An internet gateway attached to the private subnets", "A VPC gateway endpoint"], [1],
 "NAT gateways are zonal. **One per AZ**, with AZ-local routing, avoids a single point of failure and cross-AZ data charges. Gateway endpoints only reach S3 and DynamoDB."),
("net", "An application in a private subnet uploads large amounts of data to Amazon S3 in the same Region. The company wants to reduce NAT gateway data processing costs. What should they do?",
 ["Add a VPC gateway endpoint for S3", "Use S3 Transfer Acceleration", "Move the instances to a public subnet", "Add a second NAT gateway"], [0],
 "A **gateway endpoint for S3** routes traffic privately through the route table at no extra charge, bypassing the NAT gateway and its per-GB processing fee."),
("net", "A company has 30 VPCs across several accounts and an on-premises data center. They want all networks to communicate through a central hub with simple routing. Which service should they use?",
 ["VPC peering between every pair of VPCs", "AWS Transit Gateway", "A VPN connection from each VPC", "AWS PrivateLink"], [1],
 "**Transit Gateway** is a regional hub that connects many VPCs and on-premises networks (via VPN or Direct Connect) with transitive routing.\n\n- Peering isn't transitive and needs a full mesh.\n- PrivateLink exposes individual services, not whole networks."),
("net", "A company needs a dedicated, consistent-latency private connection from its data center to AWS, plus an encrypted backup path if that connection fails. What should they implement?",
 ["AWS Direct Connect with a Site-to-Site VPN as backup", "Two internet gateways", "VPC peering", "Amazon CloudFront"], [0],
 "**Direct Connect** provides dedicated, consistent bandwidth, and a **Site-to-Site VPN** over the internet is a common low-cost encrypted failover. (Direct Connect isn't encrypted by itself.)"),
("net", "A SaaS provider wants to expose a service in its VPC privately to hundreds of customer VPCs that may have overlapping CIDR ranges. Which solution fits BEST?",
 ["VPC peering", "AWS PrivateLink with a Network Load Balancer (VPC endpoint service)", "Transit Gateway", "Public Application Load Balancer"], [1],
 "**PrivateLink** exposes a service via interface endpoints in consumer VPCs. It works with overlapping CIDRs and doesn't expose the whole VPC. Peering and Transit Gateway need non-overlapping ranges."),
("net", "A company wants users to be routed to the AWS Region with the lowest latency, and automatically to another Region if one becomes unhealthy. Which Route 53 configuration should be used?",
 ["Simple routing", "Latency-based routing with health checks", "Weighted routing at 50/50 without health checks", "Geolocation routing only"], [1],
 "**Latency-based routing** sends users to the lowest-latency Region, and **health checks** remove unhealthy endpoints so traffic fails over."),
("net", "A global TCP/UDP gaming application needs static anycast IP addresses and fast regional failover, without caching. Which service should be used?",
 ["Amazon CloudFront", "AWS Global Accelerator", "Route 53 geolocation routing", "Elastic IP addresses"], [1],
 "**Global Accelerator** gives two static anycast IPs, routes over the AWS backbone, supports TCP/UDP, and fails over between Regions in seconds. CloudFront is an HTTP(S) CDN that caches content."),
# ---------- Security ----------
("sec", "A public web application is being hit by SQL injection attempts and requests from a few abusive IP ranges. Which service should be placed in front of the Application Load Balancer?",
 ["AWS Shield Standard", "AWS WAF with managed rule groups and an IP set block rule", "Security groups with deny rules", "Amazon GuardDuty"], [1],
 "**AWS WAF** inspects HTTP requests (SQLi, XSS, rate limits, IP sets) on ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway and AppSync. Security groups can't deny, Shield handles DDoS, and GuardDuty detects but doesn't block."),
("sec", "A company wants continuous threat detection that analyzes CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs for compromised instances and unusual API calls, with no agents to install. Which service should be enabled?",
 ["Amazon Inspector", "Amazon GuardDuty", "Amazon Macie", "AWS Config"], [1],
 "**GuardDuty** is agentless, ML-based threat detection over CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs.\n\n- Macie finds sensitive data in S3.\n- Inspector scans for vulnerabilities.\n- Config tracks configuration compliance."),
("sec", "The compliance team must discover which S3 buckets contain personally identifiable information (PII). Which service should be used?",
 ["Amazon Macie", "AWS Shield", "Amazon Detective", "AWS Firewall Manager"], [0],
 "**Macie** uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover and classify sensitive data such as PII in S3."),
("sec", "A website must use HTTPS with a free, auto-renewing certificate on an Application Load Balancer. What should the architect use?",
 ["Import a self-signed certificate into IAM", "AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) public certificate", "AWS KMS", "AWS CloudHSM"], [1],
 "**ACM** issues free public TLS certificates that renew automatically when attached to integrated services such as ALB, CloudFront and API Gateway."),
("sec", "A company must store encryption keys in single-tenant hardware security modules that it fully controls, to meet FIPS 140-2 Level 3 requirements. Which service should be used?",
 ["AWS KMS with AWS managed keys", "AWS CloudHSM", "AWS Secrets Manager", "SSE-S3"], [1],
 "**CloudHSM** gives dedicated, single-tenant HSMs under your exclusive control. KMS is multi-tenant and AWS-managed (though its HSMs are also FIPS validated)."),
# ---------- Compute ----------
("compute", "A nightly batch job runs for 3 hours, can be interrupted and restarted from checkpoints, and must be as cheap as possible. Which EC2 purchasing option is BEST?",
 ["On-Demand Instances", "Spot Instances", "Reserved Instances", "Dedicated Hosts"], [1],
 "**Spot Instances** offer up to 90% off and suit fault-tolerant, interruptible work, especially with checkpointing."),
("compute", "A company runs a steady 24/7 workload on EC2 but expects to change instance families and move some workloads to Fargate and Lambda over the next three years. Which option gives the biggest discount with that flexibility?",
 ["Standard Reserved Instances", "Compute Savings Plans", "EC2 Instance Savings Plans", "Spot Instances"], [1],
 "**Compute Savings Plans** apply across instance families, Regions, Fargate and Lambda. EC2 Instance Savings Plans and Standard RIs are tied to a family or Region."),
("compute", "An Auto Scaling group should keep average CPU utilization near 50% automatically. Which scaling policy requires the LEAST configuration?",
 ["Simple scaling", "Step scaling", "Target tracking scaling", "Scheduled scaling"], [2],
 "**Target tracking** works like a thermostat: you set the target metric value and it creates and manages the alarms for you."),
("compute", "A web application behind an ALB must route /api/* requests to one target group and /images/* to another. Which feature should be used?",
 ["Path-based routing rules on the ALB listener", "A Network Load Balancer", "Route 53 weighted records", "Sticky sessions"], [0],
 "ALB **listener rules** support path-based (and host-based) routing to different target groups. NLB works at Layer 4 and can't route by URL path."),
("compute", "A team wants to run containers without managing servers or clusters of EC2 instances. Which option should they choose?",
 ["Amazon ECS on EC2", "Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate", "Self-managed Kubernetes on EC2", "AWS Elastic Beanstalk with a single EC2 instance"], [1],
 "**Fargate** is serverless compute for containers (ECS or EKS). You define tasks, and AWS manages the underlying servers."),
# ---------- Architecture / resilience ----------
("arch", "A company needs a disaster recovery strategy with an RTO of minutes and a scaled-down but fully functional copy of production always running in a second Region. Which strategy is this?",
 ["Backup and restore", "Pilot light", "Warm standby", "Multi-site active/active"], [2],
 "**Warm standby** keeps a scaled-down, fully working copy running that you scale up on failover (RTO in minutes).\n\n- Pilot light runs only the core pieces, such as replicated data, so recovery is slower.\n- Active/active costs the most and has near-zero RTO."),
("arch", "A stateless web tier must survive the loss of an Availability Zone with no manual action. Which design meets this?",
 ["One large EC2 instance with an Elastic IP", "An Auto Scaling group across at least two AZs behind an Application Load Balancer", "Two instances in the same AZ behind an ALB", "An EC2 instance with a daily AMI backup"], [1],
 "An **ASG across multiple AZs behind an ALB** replaces failed instances, and the ALB only routes to healthy targets in the surviving AZs."),
("arch", "A serverless API receives sudden bursts and should return a cached response for identical GET requests for 60 seconds to reduce Lambda cost. What is the SIMPLEST solution?",
 ["Enable API Gateway caching for the stage", "Add an SQS queue in front of Lambda", "Increase Lambda memory", "Use DynamoDB Streams"], [0],
 "**API Gateway stage caching** stores responses for a TTL, so repeated requests don't invoke Lambda."),
("arch", "A company wants to decouple image processing from uploads: when a user uploads an image to S3, a thumbnail should be created automatically. Which serverless design uses the LEAST code and infrastructure?",
 ["S3 event notification that invokes an AWS Lambda function", "An EC2 instance that polls the bucket every minute", "A cron job on premises", "AWS Batch scheduled every hour"], [0],
 "**S3 event notifications → Lambda** react to each upload immediately, scale automatically, and need no servers. For extra buffering, route the event to SQS first."),
# ---------- Cost ----------
("cost", "Finance wants alerts when forecasted monthly spend for a project is expected to exceed $5,000. Which service provides this?",
 ["AWS Budgets", "AWS Cost Explorer", "AWS Pricing Calculator", "AWS Trusted Advisor"], [0],
 "**AWS Budgets** sends alerts on actual or forecasted cost or usage thresholds. Cost Explorer is for analysis, and the Pricing Calculator is for estimates before you build."),
("cost", "A company stores 200 TB in S3 with unknown and changing access patterns and does not want to manage lifecycle rules. Which storage class is MOST cost-effective?",
 ["S3 Standard", "S3 Intelligent-Tiering", "S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval", "S3 One Zone-IA"], [1],
 "**Intelligent-Tiering** automatically moves objects between access tiers based on usage, with no retrieval fees. It's ideal for unknown or changing patterns."),
("cost", "Data transfer costs are high because EC2 instances in one AZ constantly read from an RDS instance in another AZ in the same Region. What reduces cost with the LEAST change?",
 ["Place application instances in the same AZ as the database for the read path, or use a read replica in the application's AZ", "Move the database to another Region", "Use a NAT gateway", "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration"], [0],
 "Cross-AZ traffic is billed in both directions. Keeping the heavy read path **within one AZ** (co-location or a local read replica) cuts the charges. Moving Regions would increase cost."),
# ---------- Monitoring ----------
("mon", "An operations team wants to be notified when an EC2 instance's memory utilization exceeds 80%. What must be done first?",
 ["Nothing; memory is a default EC2 metric", "Install the CloudWatch agent to publish memory metrics", "Enable VPC Flow Logs", "Enable AWS Config"], [1],
 "EC2 doesn't report memory or disk-space metrics by default because those are inside the OS. The **CloudWatch agent** publishes them, and then you create an alarm."),
("mon", "Security needs a record of who deleted an S3 bucket last week and from which IP address. Where should they look?",
 ["Amazon CloudWatch metrics", "AWS CloudTrail event history", "VPC Flow Logs", "AWS Health Dashboard"], [1],
 "**CloudTrail** records API calls with the identity, time, source IP and parameters. DeleteBucket is a management event, available in Event history for 90 days."),
("mon", "A company must detect when any security group allows SSH (port 22) from 0.0.0.0/0 and automatically fix it. What is the BEST solution?",
 ["AWS Config rule with automatic remediation using an SSM Automation document", "CloudWatch dashboard", "AWS Trusted Advisor weekly report", "Amazon Inspector"], [0],
 "**AWS Config rules** continuously evaluate resource configuration, and **auto-remediation** (SSM Automation) can revoke the offending rule automatically."),
# ---------- Migration ----------
("mig", "A company must move 80 TB from an on-premises NAS to Amazon S3 within a week over a 1 Gbps internet link that is also used for business traffic. What is the MOST practical option?",
 ["AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized", "Upload with the AWS CLI over the internet", "AWS Direct Connect provisioned this week", "S3 Transfer Acceleration"], [0],
 "80 TB at 1 Gbps takes about 7.5 days at 100% utilization, and the link is shared. **Snowball Edge** ships the data physically. Direct Connect usually takes weeks to provision."),
("mig", "A company is migrating an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with minimal downtime. Which TWO services should be used? (Choose TWO)",
 ["AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT)", "AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) with ongoing replication (CDC)", "AWS DataSync", "AWS Snowcone", "AWS Application Migration Service"], [0, 1],
 "For a **heterogeneous** migration, **SCT** converts the schema and code, and **DMS** with change data capture keeps the source and target in sync until cutover. DataSync moves files, and Application Migration Service rehosts servers."),
("mig", "A company wants to lift-and-shift 200 on-premises VMware servers to EC2 with continuous replication and a short cutover window. Which service should they use?",
 ["AWS Application Migration Service (MGN)", "AWS DMS", "AWS DataSync", "AWS Backup"], [0],
 "**Application Migration Service** continuously replicates whole servers at block level and launches them as EC2 instances at cutover, which is the recommended rehost tool."),
# ---------- Analytics ----------
("ana", "Analysts want to run ad hoc SQL queries directly on CSV and Parquet files in S3 without loading them into a database or managing servers. Which service should they use?",
 ["Amazon Athena", "Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster", "Amazon RDS", "Amazon EMR on EC2"], [0],
 "**Athena** is serverless SQL over data in S3, billed per TB scanned. Columnar formats like Parquet and partitioning reduce cost."),
("ana", "IoT devices send thousands of records per second that must be delivered to S3 in near real time, converted to Parquet, with no custom consumer code. Which service fits BEST?",
 ["Amazon Data Firehose", "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with a custom KCL application", "Amazon SQS", "AWS Glue batch jobs every hour"], [0],
 "**Amazon Data Firehose** (formerly Kinesis Data Firehose) is fully managed delivery to S3, Redshift or OpenSearch, with buffering and built-in format conversion to Parquet or ORC. Kinesis Data Streams needs consumer code."),
]
